-- Q11548: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IAS 36.117, impairment losses on assets other than goodwill may be reversed when conditions improve, but the reversed carrying amount cannot exceed what it would have been (net of depreciation) had no impairment been recognized. Under ASC 360-10-35-20, U.S. GAAP prohibits reversal of impairment losses on long-lived assets.
Wrong (A): U.S. GAAP prohibits reversal entirely. Only IFRS permits reversal, and even then it is subject to a depreciation-adjusted cap, not the original carrying amount.
Wrong (B): IFRS does permit reversal for assets other than goodwill when conditions improve. Only U.S. GAAP prohibits reversal of long-lived asset impairment losses.
Wrong (C): The IFRS reversal cap is the depreciation-adjusted carrying amount, not the full original carrying amount. Reversing to $3,200,000 would overstate the asset.$EXPL$
