-- Q11582: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 230-10-45-15, dividends paid to shareholders are financing activities representing returns to owners. Under ASC 230-10-45-28, dividends received from investments are operating activities representing returns on investments analogous to interest income.
Wrong (A): Dividends received are not financing activities under U.S. GAAP. They are classified as operating cash flows because they represent investment income.
Wrong (C): Dividends paid are not operating activities. They represent distributions to owners, which are financing cash flows, not part of ongoing operations.
Wrong (D): Under U.S. GAAP, dividends paid are financing activities (not operating), and dividends received are operating activities (not investing).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11582;

-- Q11583: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 210-10-45, working capital is defined as current assets minus current liabilities and measures an entity''s short-term liquidity position. For Ashford, working capital is $820,000 minus $530,000, or $290,000.
Wrong (A): Total assets minus total liabilities equals total stockholders'' equity under the accounting equation, not working capital. Working capital focuses only on current items.
Wrong (B): Net income plus depreciation approximates operating cash flow, not working capital. Working capital is a balance sheet measure, not an income statement calculation.
Wrong (C): Cash minus short-term debt is a narrower liquidity measure. Working capital includes all current assets and all current liabilities, not just cash and debt.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11583;

-- Q11584: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 230-10-45, the indirect method starts with net income and adjusts for noncash items and working capital changes. Net income $320,000 + depreciation $45,000 + amortization $12,000 - decrease in accounts payable $18,000 = $359,000.
Wrong (A): $377,000 incorrectly adds the accounts payable decrease instead of subtracting it. A decrease in payables means cash was used to pay suppliers, reducing operating cash flow.
Wrong (C): $395,000 likely double-counts an adjustment or omits the payable decrease. Only the three adjustments listed produce the correct $359,000 total.
Wrong (D): $341,000 likely subtracts an additional item or misapplies the depreciation and amortization add-backs. Both noncash charges must be added back to net income.$EXPL$
